\title{EML Transseries: A Hahn-Series Field Model and the\\ Asymptotic Comparison Theorem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-23}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Transseries extend classical power series by admitting iterated exponentials and logarithms as building blocks, providing a closed asymptotic language for the growth of exp-log-monomial (EML) functions at $+\infty$. We construct the field of transseries concretely as a Hahn series field $\TSeries = \Hahn\big(\Lex(\Z \to_{0} \R),\, \R\big)$, whose value group of \emph{transmonomials} is the lexicographically ordered group of finitely supported maps from integer \textbf{heights} (the number of iterated exponentials, negative for logarithms) to real \textbf{exponents}. The Hahn construction endows this object with a complete field structure together with a valuation $\orderTop$ valued in $\WithTop(\TransMono)$, the leading transmonomial of a series. Our central result is the \textbf{asymptotic comparison theorem}: two transseries that \emph{agree to all orders} --- meaning the valuation of their difference exceeds every transmonomial --- are necessarily equal, and conversely. This is the uniqueness principle that distinguishes transseries from ordinary asymptotic expansions of real functions, which are not determined by their expansions (witness $e^{-1/x^2}$). We show the agreement relation is an equivalence relation coinciding with equality, derive the contrapositive that no nonzero series agrees to all orders with $0$, and anchor the abstract height order to real analysis via two dominance facts: $x^n = o(e^x)$ and $(e^x)^n = o(e^{e^x})$ as $x \to +\infty$. Finally, we build a bridge between this principled field model and a combinatorial ``level-then-exponent'' dominance relation, proving the two orders coincide precisely on positive-exponent monomials and exhibiting the negative-exponent regime where the naive relation fails. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
Asymptotic analysis asks how quantities behave in a limit, and its answers are \emph{rates of growth} rather than numbers. The classical instrument, the power series $\sum_n a_n x^n$, is incapable of representing transcendental growth: $e^x$ requires infinitely many powers and $e^{e^x}$ is outright unreachable. Yet such towers of exponentials and logarithms saturate combinatorics (set partitions grow like $e^{e^x}$), the analysis of algorithms, statistical mechanics, and the asymptotics of solutions to differential equations.

\textbf{Transseries} are the closure of formal power series under the operations that generate these objects: exponentiation, taking logarithms, and infinite (well-ordered) summation. They form a field in which every elementary growth rate has a canonical normal form, an exact arithmetic, and --- the property we focus on here --- a \emph{uniqueness} guarantee: a transseries is determined by its asymptotic expansion to all orders.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item A concrete field model of transseries as a Hahn series field over a lexicographically ordered group of transmonomials (Section~\ref{sec:field}).
\item The \textbf{asymptotic comparison theorem} (Theorem~\ref{thm:comparison}): agreeing to all orders is equivalent to equality.
\item Structural corollaries: the agreement relation is an equivalence relation (Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv}), and no nonzero series agrees to all orders with $0$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:nonzero}).
\item Analytic grounding of the formal height order via two little-$o$ dominance results (Section~\ref{sec:analytic}).
\item A bridge theorem identifying a combinatorial dominance relation with the field's lexicographic order on positive-exponent monomials (Section~\ref{sec:bridge}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to ordinary asymptotic expansions}
The motivating contrast is \textbf{non-uniqueness for real functions}. The function
\[
\phi(x) = \begin{cases} e^{-1/x^2}, & x \neq 0, \\ 0, & x = 0, \end{cases}
\]
is smooth and is $o(x^n)$ near $0$ for every $n$, so its Taylor series at $0$ is identically zero --- the \emph{same} expansion as the zero function, although $\phi \neq 0$. Real asymptotic expansions thus fail to determine the function. The asymptotic comparison theorem says transseries suffer no such defect: distinct transseries always diverge at some finite, nameable order.

\section{Preliminaries: Hahn series and valuations}

\begin{definition}[Hahn series]
Let $(\Gamma, +, <)$ be a linearly ordered abelian group and $R$ a ring. A \emph{Hahn series} over $\Gamma$ with coefficients in $R$ is a function $f : \Gamma \to R$ whose support $\supp(f) = \{ g \in \Gamma : f(g) \neq 0 \}$ is \textbf{well-ordered}. The set of all such series is denoted $\Hahn(\Gamma, R)$. Addition is pointwise; multiplication is the convolution
\[
(fg)(\gamma) = \sum_{\alpha + \beta = \gamma} f(\alpha)\, g(\beta),
\]
which is well-defined because the well-ordering of supports makes each such sum finite.
\end{definition}

\begin{fact}
If $R$ is a field and $\Gamma$ is a linearly ordered abelian group, then $\Hahn(\Gamma, R)$ is a field. Inverses are computed by a formally convergent geometric expansion controlled by the well-ordering.
\end{fact}

\begin{definition}[Order valuation]
For $f \in \Hahn(\Gamma, R)$, the \emph{order} (or top-valuation) is
\[
\orderTop(f) = \begin{cases} \min \supp(f) \in \Gamma, & f \neq 0, \\ \top, & f = 0, \end{cases}
\]
valued in $\WithTop(\Gamma) = \Gamma \cup \{\top\}$, where $\top$ is a maximal element strictly above every $g \in \Gamma$. The minimum exists because the support is well-ordered. By convention, a \emph{smaller} element of $\Gamma$ corresponds to a \emph{more dominant} growth rate, so $\orderTop(f)$ records the leading transmonomial of $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{fact}[Valuation laws]\label{fact:val}
The order valuation satisfies:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\orderTop(f) = \top \iff f = 0$;
\item $\orderTop(fg) = \orderTop(f) + \orderTop(g)$;
\item $\orderTop(f + g) \ge \min(\orderTop(f), \orderTop(g))$.
\end{itemize}
The first law is the cornerstone of the comparison theorem.
\end{fact}

\section{The transmonomial group and the transseries field}\label{sec:field}

\subsection{Heights and exponents}
The elementary growth rates are organized by an integer \textbf{height} counting iterated exponentials:
\[
\dots,\ \log\log x\ (h=-2),\ \log x\ (h=-1),\ x\ (h=0),\ e^x\ (h=1),\ e^{e^x}\ (h=2),\ \dots
\]

\begin{definition}[Transmonomial group]
A \emph{transmonomial} is a finitely supported function from heights to real exponents:
\[
\TransMono = \Lex\big(\Z \to_{0} \R\big) = \{\, m : \Z \to \R \mid m(h) \neq 0 \text{ for finitely many } h \,\},
\]
an abelian group under pointwise addition of exponents (corresponding to multiplication of monomials), ordered \textbf{lexicographically by height from the top down}: the tallest tower wins, and within a tier the larger exponent wins.
\end{definition}

We write $\mono(h, a) \in \TransMono$ for the \emph{pure transmonomial} assigning exponent $a$ to height $h$ and $0$ elsewhere; it represents $\big(\exp^{\circ h}(x)\big)^a$ for $h \ge 0$. Thus $\mono(0, \alpha) = x^\alpha$, $\mono(1, \beta) = e^{\beta x}$, $\mono(2, 1) = e^{e^x}$.

\begin{lemma}[Lexicographic comparison]\label{lem:lex}
For pure transmonomials of distinct heights $h_1 < h_2$ with nonzero exponents, $\mono(h_1, a) < \mono(h_2, b)$ whenever $b > 0$. For equal heights, $\mono(h, a) < \mono(h, b) \iff a < b$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{The field of transseries}
\begin{definition}[Transseries]
The field of transseries is the Hahn series field over the transmonomial group with real coefficients:
\[
\TSeries = \Hahn\big(\TransMono,\, \R\big).
\]
An element is a formal sum $\sum_m c_m \cdot m$ with $c_m \in \R$ and well-ordered support; $\TSeries$ is a field. The order valuation $\orderTop : \TSeries \to \WithTop(\TransMono)$ returns the leading transmonomial of a nonzero series and $\top$ for $0$.
\end{definition}

\section{Agreeing to all orders}

\begin{definition}[Agreement to all orders]
Two transseries $a, b \in \TSeries$ \emph{agree to all orders}, written $\AgreeToAllOrders(a, b)$, when
\[
\AgreeToAllOrders(a, b) :\equiv \forall\, g \in \TransMono,\quad (g : \WithTop\,\TransMono) < \orderTop(a - b).
\]
Interpreted asymptotically: $a - b$ is smaller than every named growth rate. The quantifier ranges over the entire (uncountable) transmonomial group.
\end{definition}

\section{The Asymptotic Comparison Theorem}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:top}
For $f \in \TSeries$, $\orderTop(f) = \top \iff f = 0$. \emph{(First valuation law, Fact~\ref{fact:val}.)}
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic comparison theorem]\label{thm:comparison}
For all $a, b \in \TSeries$,
\[
\AgreeToAllOrders(a, b) \iff a = b.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Assume $a$ and $b$ agree to all orders. We claim $\orderTop(a - b) = \top$. Suppose not; then $\orderTop(a-b)$ is finite, so there exists $c \in \TransMono$ with $\orderTop(a - b) = (c)$. Instantiating the hypothesis at $g = c$ gives $(c) < \orderTop(a-b) = (c)$, contradicting irreflexivity. Hence $\orderTop(a - b) = \top$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:top}, $a - b = 0$, i.e. $a = b$.

($\Leftarrow$) If $a = b$, then $a - b = 0$, so $\orderTop(a-b) = \top > (g)$ for every $g$ since $\top$ is maximal. Thus $a$ and $b$ agree to all orders.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The proof is short but not vacuous: the forward direction must eliminate \emph{every} possible finite leading rate via the case analysis on $\WithTop$. The mathematical weight resides in the order structure of $\TransMono$ and in Lemma~\ref{lem:top}.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Reflexivity]
$\AgreeToAllOrders(a, a)$ holds for all $a$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Equivalence relation]\label{thm:equiv}
$\AgreeToAllOrders$ is an equivalence relation on $\TSeries$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:comparison} the relation coincides extensionally with equality, which is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive; each property transfers through the biconditional.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No nonzero series is asymptotically negligible]\label{prop:nonzero}
If $a \neq 0$ then $\neg\,\AgreeToAllOrders(a, 0)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $\AgreeToAllOrders(a, 0)$ held, then $a = 0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:comparison}, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Analytic grounding of the height order}\label{sec:analytic}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential dominates powers]\label{thm:powexp}
For every $n \in \N$,
\[
x^{n} = o\big(e^{x}\big) \quad \text{as } x \to +\infty.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Double exponential dominates powers of the exponential]\label{thm:expexp}
For every $n \in \N$,
\[
\big(e^{x}\big)^{n} = o\big(e^{e^{x}}\big) \quad \text{as } x \to +\infty.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:powexp} in the variable $u = e^x$: $u^n = o(e^u)$ as $u \to +\infty$. Compose with $e^x \to +\infty$ to pull the limit back along $x \mapsto e^x$, yielding $(e^x)^n = o(e^{e^x})$.
\end{proof}

\section{A bridge to combinatorial dominance}\label{sec:bridge}

\begin{definition}[Catalog transmonomial and dominance]
A \emph{catalog transmonomial} is a record with fields $\mathrm{level} \in \Z$ and $\mathrm{exponent} \in \R$. The combinatorial dominance relation is
\[
\domRel(m_1, m_2) :\equiv m_1.\mathrm{level} < m_2.\mathrm{level} \ \lor\ \big(m_1.\mathrm{level} = m_2.\mathrm{level} \ \land\ m_1.\mathrm{exponent} < m_2.\mathrm{exponent}\big).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Embedding]
$\embed(m) = \mono(m.\mathrm{level},\, m.\mathrm{exponent}) \in \TransMono.$
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge on positive monomials]\label{thm:bridge}
For catalog transmonomials whose dominant exponents are positive,
\[
\domRel(m_1, m_2) \iff \embed(m_1) < \embed(m_2).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Easy direction]
Without positivity on the larger monomial, $\domRel(m_1, m_2)$ implies $\embed(m_1) < \embed(m_2)$ in the cases where the level strictly increases or exponents compare within a level.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[Why positivity is load-bearing]
The full equivalence fails for negative dominant exponents. Consider $(e^x)^{-1} = e^{-x}$ (level $1$, exponent $-1$): the level-first $\domRel$ ranks it above every power of $x$, yet $e^{-x}$ \emph{decays} and is dominated by $x$. The positivity condition marks exactly the boundary where the naive rule is correct.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Transmonomial comparison.} Scan the union of supports from the highest height downward; the first height with differing exponents decides the order. Complexity $O(k \log k)$ for total support size $k$.

\paragraph{Order-by-order agreement test.} Compute $d = a - b$; if $d = 0$, agree; else return whether the leading transmonomial of $d$ dominates all monomials up to a cutoff height $G$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:comparison}, $a = b$ iff agreement holds for every $G$.

\section{Applications}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Exact asymptotics for differential equations:} term-by-term formal solutions are unique by Theorem~\ref{thm:comparison}.
\item \textbf{Resurgence and exponential asymptotics:} exponentially small terms beyond all orders of a divergent power series live at a finite order in the transmonomial hierarchy.
\item \textbf{Model theory of $o$-minimal structures:} uniqueness of expansion is a prerequisite for universality of transseries.
\item \textbf{Algorithmic asymptotics:} deciding equality and dominance of EML functions via finite truncations.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and Future Directions}

\begin{conjecture}[The transseries field is real closed]
$\Hahn(\Lex(\Z \to_0 \R), \R)$, ordered by leading coefficient, admits square roots of all positive elements and roots of all odd-degree polynomials, hence is real closed. Root-finding is governed by the Newton polygon over the valuation, reducing to solvability of the leading-coefficient equation in $\R$ plus a fixpoint iteration. The two facts a Newton-polygon argument needs --- multiplicativity of $\orderTop$ and $\orderTop(x) = \top \iff x = 0$ --- are established here.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Valuation isometry]
The leading-data map $x \mapsto (\orderTop(x), \leadingCoeff(x))$ is a surjective valuation onto $\TransMono \times \R^\times$; two transseries are equal iff all truncations $\{m : m \le g\}$ agree for every $g$. The comparison theorem is the $g \to \infty$ colimit of decidable truncated equalities.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Exp/log shift automorphism]
The height shift $\mono(h, a) \mapsto \mono(h+1, a)$ extends to a strictly order-preserving group automorphism of $\TransMono$, and conjugating the Hahn construction by it models the substitution $x \mapsto e^x$.
\end{conjecture}

A \emph{full} transseries field closed under composition and derivation requires an infinite-rank value group (a transfinite tower) beyond the single-tower $\Z \to_0 \R$ model used here.

\section{Conclusion}
We have presented a concrete Hahn-series field model of EML transseries, organized by an integer height hierarchy and ordered lexicographically. The asymptotic comparison theorem establishes that a transseries is uniquely determined by its expansion to all orders --- the uniqueness principle that ordinary real asymptotic expansions lack. The result rests on two valuation laws, is anchored to real analysis by exponential-dominance limits, and connects faithfully to combinatorial dominance on the positive-exponent regime.

